        "step_anchor": "step-1",
        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example, suppose that we wish to simplify <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.]</span> To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.</p>",
            "readable_text": "For example, suppose that we wish to simplify [MATH: \\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.] To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n&amp;=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&amp;=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&amp;=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&amp;=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n&=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nContent:\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify [MATH: \\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.] To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n&=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n}]"
      },
