    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nContent:\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify [MATH: \\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.] To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n&=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Rational Expressions With Common Constant Factors\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is [MATH: \\dfrac {2x + 4} {2}] reduced to lowest terms?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n[MATH: (2x + 4)/(2)|= (2(x + 2))/(2); = (2(x + 2))/(2 \u22c5 1); = (x + 2)/(1); = x + 2]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is [MATH: (4z \u2212 8x)/(2z \u2212 4y)] reduced to its lowest terms?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (z + 2x)/(z + 2y)]\nb. [MATH: (z \u2212 2x)/(z \u2212 2y)]\nc. [MATH: 2]\nd. [MATH: 4]\ne. [MATH: (2(z \u2212 2x))/(z \u2212 2y)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (6b + 6a)/(6a) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6b]\nb. [MATH: b + a]\nc. [MATH: b]\nd. [MATH: b + 1]\ne. [MATH: (b + a)/(a)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Rational Expressions With Common Factors\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x} &= \\dfrac {6y + 2} {x(3y + 1)} \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2(3y + 1)} {x(3y + 1)} \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2\\cancel{(3y + 1)} } {x \\cancel{(3y + 1)} } \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 } {x}\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (4xy \u2212 x)/(4y \u2212 1) =]\n\nChoices:\na. Not reducible\nb. [MATH: xy]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(y)]\nd. [MATH: (x)/(y)]\ne. [MATH: x]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (ab \u2212 b)/(3a \u2212 3) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (b)/(a)]\nb. Not reducible\nc. [MATH: (b)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: (3)/(b)]\ne. [MATH: (a)/(b)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Identifying Irreducible Rational Expressions\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is [MATH: \\dfrac{x+2}{2x}] reduced to lowest terms?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe numerator and denominator have no common factors, so the expression can not be reduced. The expression [MATH: \\dfrac{x+2}{2x}] is already in lowest terms.\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (7x + 1)/(7x) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 7]\nb. [MATH: 1]\nc. [MATH: 7x]\nd. [MATH: (7x + 1)/(7x)]\ne. [MATH: 7x + 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (3a + 4)/(2) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2]\nb. [MATH: 3a + 2]\nc. [MATH: 3a + 4]\nd. [MATH: (3a + 4)/(2)]\ne. [MATH: (3a)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Rational Expressions With Longer Numerators and Denominators\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\dfrac{5c + 10p - 20}{5p+15}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n[MATH: (5c + 10p \u2212 20)/(5p + 15)|= (5(c + 2p \u2212 4))/(5(p + 3)); = (5(c + 2p \u2212 4))/(5(p + 3)); = (c + 2p \u2212 4)/(p + 3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 12 \u2014 Question 7\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is [MATH: (4ab \u2212 8 + 10x)/(10x + 8 \u2212 2ab)] reduced to its lowest terms?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 2]\nb. [MATH: (ab \u2212 2 + 5x)/(5x + 2 \u2212 ab)]\nc. [MATH: (ab \u2212 4 + 5x)/(5x + 4 \u2212 ab)]\nd. [MATH: (2ab \u2212 4 + 5x)/(5x + 4 \u2212 ab)]\ne. [MATH: 2]\n\n---\n\nItem 13 \u2014 Question 8\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is [MATH: (8a \u2212 4b + 12)/(20b + 8a)] reduced to its lowest terms?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (2a \u2212 b + 3)/(5b + 2a)]\nb. [MATH: (2a \u2212 b)/(5b + 2a)]\nc. [MATH: (\u2212 b + 5)/(4b)]\nd. [MATH: (\u2212 4b + 12)/(20b)]\ne. [MATH: (2a + b \u2212 3)/(5b \u2212 2a)]"
  }
